FIVE PROJECT TASKS 
Azure Hybrid Identity with Password Hash Synchronisation 
The following five tasks represent the major implementation activities of the project. Each task has a defined 
objective, implementation scope and expected result. Together, they cover the complete process of building, 
configuring and validating the hybrid identity environment. 
Task 1 — Set Up the Azure Environment 
Objective: Establish the Azure infrastructure required to host the hybrid identity laboratory. 
Detailed Explanation: The first task is to prepare a controlled Azure environment for the project. An 
appropriate Azure subscription is selected and a dedicated resource group is created to keep all project 
resources together. A virtual network and subnet are configured for the Windows Server virtual machine. A 
Windows Server VM is then deployed with the resources required for the identity lab. Network access is 
configured so that the server can be administered and can communicate with the required Microsoft identity 
services. The deployment should be kept to the minimum resources necessary for the academic demonstration 
to reduce unnecessary Azure usage. 
Expected Result: A functioning Windows Server VM is available inside the Azure environment and is ready for 
Active Directory and Microsoft Entra Connect configuration. 
Task 2 — Configure Active Directory Domain Services 
Objective: Create the on-premises identity source that will be synchronized with Microsoft Entra ID. 
Detailed Explanation: Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) is installed on the Windows Server and 
configured as the domain controller for the laboratory domain. A test domain is established and appropriate 
organizational units (OUs), security groups and user accounts are created. Test users represent the identities 
that an organization would normally maintain in its on-premises directory. The directory structure is kept 
organized so that synchronization filtering can be demonstrated later. User credentials are configured for the 
test accounts and the domain is verified before proceeding to cloud synchronization. 
Expected Result: A working Active Directory domain exists with test users, groups and OUs that can serve as 
the source directory for the hybrid identity configuration. 
Task 3 — Configure Microsoft Entra Connect and Password Hash Synchronisation 
Objective: Establish synchronization between Active Directory and Microsoft Entra ID using Password Hash 
Synchronisation. 
Detailed Explanation: Microsoft Entra Connect is installed on the Windows Server and configured to connect 
the local Active Directory environment to the Microsoft Entra tenant. During configuration, the required 
administrative credentials and directory connection information are provided. Password Hash Synchronisation 
is selected as the authentication method. Synchronization scope and filtering are configured so that only the 
intended directory objects are included. An initial synchronization is then performed and its status is checked. 
Password Hash Synchronisation synchronizes password-derived information rather than transferring the user's 
original plaintext password to Microsoft Entra ID. 
Expected Result: Microsoft Entra Connect successfully connects the two identity environments and begins 
synchronizing the selected Active Directory objects and password-derived information to Microsoft Entra ID. 
Task 4 — Synchronize and Verify User Identities 
Objective: Confirm that synchronized identities are correctly created in Microsoft Entra ID and can be used for 
cloud authentication. 
Detailed Explanation: The fourth task validates the synchronization process. Selected test users and groups are 
synchronized from Active Directory and checked in the Microsoft Entra admin environment. Important identity 
attributes are reviewed to confirm that the synchronized objects correspond to the correct on-premises 
accounts. Synchronization status and results are examined for errors or unexpected objects. A controlled sign-in 
test is then performed using a synchronized test account to verify that Password Hash Synchronisation is 
functioning as expected. Screenshots and test observations can be collected as evidence for the project report. 
Expected Result: Selected Active Directory users and groups are visible in Microsoft Entra ID, synchronization 
completes successfully, and the test user can authenticate to the intended cloud service using the synchronized 
credentials. 
Task 5 — Configure Filtering and Troubleshoot Synchronization 
Objective: Control synchronization scope and demonstrate the identification and resolution of common hybrid 
identity problems. 
Detailed Explanation: The final task focuses on operational control and troubleshooting. Synchronization 
filtering is configured so that only the required users, groups or organizational units are synchronized. The 
project then examines common failure scenarios such as duplicate attributes, conflicting identity information, 
incorrect filtering rules, synchronization failures and authentication problems. Microsoft Entra Connect 
synchronization status and available diagnostic information are reviewed to identify the cause of an issue. 
Configuration is corrected and synchronization is repeated to confirm that the problem has been resolved. This 
task demonstrates that a hybrid identity environment requires both correct initial configuration and ongoing 
monitoring. 
Expected Result: Only intended identities are synchronized, common synchronization issues can be identified 
and corrected, and the final environment operates with the expected identity and authentication behaviour. 
Overall Task Flow 
Azure Infrastructure → Active Directory → Entra Connect + PHS → Synchronization & Authentication 
Testing → Filtering & Troubleshooting 
